---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { ROLLS } from '../calculators/wallpaper';
import { wallpaperRolls } from '../lib/finish';

const rooms: [number, number][] = [[10, 10], [10, 12], [12, 12], [12, 14], [14, 16], [16, 20]];
const table = rooms.map(([l, w]) => {
  const room = { wallWidthsFt: [l, w, l, w], heightFt: 8, rollWidthIn: ROLLS[0].w, rollLengthFt: ROLLS[0].l };
  return {
    l,
    w,
    plain: wallpaperRolls({ ...room, repeatIn: 0, match: 'plain' }).rolls,
    straight: wallpaperRolls({ ...room, repeatIn: 21, match: 'straight' }).rolls,
  };
});
---

<CalcPage
  slug="wallpaper-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Wallpaper Calculator: Rolls & Pattern Repeat"
  description="Estimate wallpaper rolls from room size, roll dimensions, and pattern repeat. Compare plain and straight-match cuts, strips per roll, and trim."
  h1="Wallpaper Calculator"
  updated="2026-11-19"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Estimate how many rolls of wallpaper you need. Enter the room and roll dimensions, then choose the pattern match
    from the roll label to see how many full-height strips you can cut.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate wallpaper rolls</h2>
  <p>
    Count the strips each wall needs, then count how many fit on a roll after trimming and matching the design.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Divide each wall's width by the roll width and round up separately. This estimate starts each wall with a fresh strip.</li>
    <li>Add 4 inches to the wall height for trimming. Use the roll label to choose how to align the cuts.</li>
    <li>Divide the total number of strips by the number that fit on a roll, then round up to a whole roll.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 12 × 12 ft room with 8 ft walls</h2>
  <p>
    Each 12 ft wall is 144 in wide. With 20.5 in paper it takes 8 strips per wall, or 32 for the room. With plain paper,
    each strip is 96 + 4 = 100 in long. A 33 ft roll gives 3 full strips, so 32 ÷ 3 rounds up to <strong>11 rolls</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Rolls for common room sizes</h2>
  <p>8 ft walls and 20.5 in × 33 ft rolls. The estimate counts full-height strips on each wall.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room</th><th class="num">Plain paper</th><th class="num">21 in straight match</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{table.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.l} × {r.w} ft</td><td class="num">{r.plain}</td><td class="num">{r.straight}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="repeat">How pattern match changes the cuts</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern repeat is the vertical distance between matching points in the design. With a straight match, each
    strip starts at the same point in that repeat. For a random match, you can start the next strip right after the previous cut.
    For a drop match or an unknown match, the calculator budgets one full repeat per strip. Check the product's layout
    instructions for alternating patterns. See the match types in
    <a href="https://yorkwallcoverings.com/pages/installation-guide">York's wallpaper installation guide</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Three straight-match strips from one roll</h3>
  <p>
    For a 9 ft wall, a strip with trim measures 112 in. With a 21 in straight repeat, the next matching start can be
    126 in away (6 repeats). Allow 21 in at the start of the roll to find the first match. You could then start cuts at
    21, 147, and 273 in. The third ends at 385 in, inside a 396 in roll. Choose <strong>Straight match</strong> and enter
    a 21 in repeat to see 3 strips per roll.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roll-label">Check the roll label before ordering</h2>
  <p>
    Use the dimensions of the physical roll you receive, not just a listing's price unit. A 20.5 in × 33 ft double roll
    is twice the length of a 20.5 in × 16.5 ft single roll. The calculator's cost input is per selected physical roll.
    Confirm the package quantity with the seller, and choose rolls from the same dye lot.
  </p>

  <p>
    Papering only an accent wall or around sloped ceilings? <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">Measure the wall area</a> and compare it with
    the usable area on the roll label.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How much does a roll of wallpaper cover?</h3>
  <p>
    A 20.5 in × 33 ft double roll is about 56 square feet before trimming. For an 8 ft wall and 4 in of trim, that roll
    gives three plain strips, covering about 41 ft² of wall. The actual coverage depends on the height and match.
  </p>
  <h3>Should I subtract doors and windows?</h3>
  <p>
    Door and window counts reduce the displayed net wall area. The roll estimate counts full strips because you still
    need paper above doors and around windows. If you plan to reuse offcuts or skip a full-height opening, draw its
    position on the wall and check which cuts it actually replaces.
  </p>
  <p>Patching walls before papering? The <a href="/drywall-calculator/">drywall calculator</a> covers new sheets.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Strips per wall = wall width ÷ roll width, rounded up. The widths are measured in the same unit.</li>
    <li>For straight matches, round the distance from one cut start to the next up to a whole repeat. The last strip only needs its cut length.</li>
    <li>Rolls = strips ÷ strips per roll, rounded up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
